── the independent oracle ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  integration/oracle/ shares no code with the contract. `device_vector.py` is a SIGNER: it writes
  a device's attendance batch again from the specification in `AttFold.batchBytes` and signs it
  with `cryptography`'s own P-256, so the attendance battery verifies a batch this tree did not
  sign - and refuses one whose punches were changed by a minute, one replayed at a counter already
  spent, and one signed by another device's key. `webauthn_vector.py` is a SIGNER too: it
  recomputes the contract's signing challenge from the specification and produces a real
  WebAuthn ES256 assertion with `cryptography`'s own P-256 implementation, so the evidence
  battery verifies a signature this tree did not make; with `--sign` it produces two signers'
  assertions in the order the signing battery (`test/Sign.test.mo`) names them. `sign.py` is the
  signing flow's READER: it decodes the battery's log with the enterprise envelope, verifies every
  assertion with `cryptography` over the challenge it recomputed, and recomputes every seal;
  `evidence.py` decodes the evidence families for it. `stock.py` decodes and re-encodes
  the inventory families and REFOLDS the stock: quants, first-in-first-out layers, the
  average pair, the standard, and every validated picking's value by Python's own
  `decimal` at the reference precision, handing the valuation entry to the ledger
  reader. `crm.py` decodes and re-encodes every CRM family and REFOLDS the pipeline's
  mailing and campaign: the leads and their tags, the suppression list, every mailing's
  recipients as planned, claimed and acknowledged, the segment's rule, the audience dealt
  into arms by its own statement of the recorded rule, the winner by its own
  cross-multiplication, and the remainder; and the pipeline itself - every lead's state,
  stage, board state and weighted forecast, the reminder sweep over the activities the
  call reader keeps, and the scoring run by its own statement of the nine criteria.
  `typed_codec.py` decodes and re-encodes a command of every family from the command type
  and the enumerations' stored bytes alone, and every block on the log is written back by
  it. `accounting.py` REFOLDS the
  month-end close from its own ledger - the plan as every profit-and-loss account's
  period totals in account order and its hash, the sweep as those accounts brought to
  zero with the difference to retained earnings in moves of at most 254 lines, the
  reversal as each closing move's lines swapped - and compares the contract's `close:`
  line figure by figure; it REFOLDS every statement over its window, hashes it with the
  block and the tip it names and requires that tip to be the hash of the block before,
  comparing the `statement:` lines; it REFOLDS every deferral's schedule with its own calendar
  arithmetic and integer division and generates the run's moves in the same order, comparing
  the `deferral:` lines to the minor unit; it REFOLDS every revaluation under the declared
  exponents and rounding with its own half-even arithmetic and the rate it finds in force,
  comparing the `revaluation:` lines; it REFOLDS every asset's charges by its own rule - what is
  left over the months that are left - and every event's result against the carrying value it
  keeps, comparing the `asset:` lines to the minor unit. `expenses.py` decodes and re-encodes the
  eight expense families, notes the documents the evidence families register, archive and erase,
  REPRICES every rate line from the rate it finds in force on the line's day with its own half-even
  arithmetic, sums every total, folds the claim's state machine with its three pairs of eyes, hands
  the approval's and the settlement's moves to the ledger reader, and compares the `claim:` lines.
  `payments.py` decodes and re-encodes the payment families and the three-way match's, folds the
  bill from its lines and hands the match's move to the ledger reader, REBUILDS every pacs.008 from
  the rows with its own writer - its own MOD 97-10, its own UETR derivation, its own civil date and
  amount - and requires the same bytes under the same SHA-256, posting the acceptance's move itself,
  comparing the `payment:` lines. `statements.py` decodes and re-encodes the statement families,
  typed and from the message, READS every camt.053 again with Python's own parser into the same
  statement and lines, holds the message to the official camt.053.001.08 schema with lxml where the
  hub's copy of it is beside this tree, and requires the bytes to be the fixture file they say they
  are, comparing the `camt:` lines. `esg.py` decodes and re-encodes the ESG families, folds every GWP set,
  factor version, rule and activity by its own statement of the rules, WALKS every run slice by slice over the
  accounting reader's moves and its own activities with its own half-even arithmetic, chains and hashes every
  sealed run with Python's own SHA-256, and writes every disclosure and the FRA's answers again, comparing the
  `esg_*` lines whole. `accounting.py` additionally emits a Beancount ledger and an hledger
  journal; Beancount (`bean-check`) is run over the first, and a control requires it
  to REFUSE the same ledger with one posting moved by a minor unit; where the `hledger`
  binary is installed it reads the second and must arrive at every balance, the swept
  accounts at zero among them. Beancount is GPL-2.0 and hledger is GPL-3.0; both are
  used as external tools, not vendored.
  `read_page.py` decodes a page of the read method from its bytes alone, by the codec's rules and
  the schema `tools/read_surface.py` writes from the declarations (`read_kinds.json`: each kind's
  fields, how each is written, and every enumeration's bytes by name), and refuses a page it cannot
  read to its last byte or that is not the one spelling of what it holds. `read_compare.py` holds
  the export's pages - every kind in every company walked to its end - to the log's height and the
  tip this reader chained, to one walk per kind and company, to the company of each row, and to this
  reader's own fold both ways, field by field, under a floor (`read_floor.json`) that a run may not
  fall below.

